# ── Chapter 19 fidelity pins: "What May Be Kept About You" ─────────────────
#
# KIND: CONTENT pin. Run:
#   ./verify.sh --only book-1/19-what-may-be-kept-about-you.pins.nibli
#   ./verify.sh          # the real check — the line above cannot see
#                        # the cross-file reconciliation or the prose guards
#
# The chapter's case lists two fixtures:
#   tests/pins/records/child_with_nobody/fixture.nibli
#   tests/pins/record-power/power/holding-defect-and-rights-sequence
# The second builds a reviewed holding, the watching that rejoins it, and the
# automated support that rejoins both, with a person standing beside them.
#
# The rest of the two families' coverage is cited rather than duplicated:
#   tests/pins/record-power/power/holding-does-not-cover-another-subject
#                                       a holding reviewed about one person
#                                       licenses nothing about another
#   tests/pins/record-power/power/unrelated-defect   a defect stops the exact
#                                       use it names and leaves the rest
#   tests/pins/record-power/{holding,processing,automated,access,retention,
#                            nonresponse,defect}/   the field, attester,
#                                       vocabulary, version and period matrix
#   tests/pins/statistics/{use,pattern,rebuttal,data-wall,access,challenge,
#                          release,nonresponse,defect}/   the statistical
#                                       interface, its remedial-proceeding
#                                       presumption and its criminal-burden
#                                       wall
#
# WHAT FALSE MEANS HERE: a FALSE is pinned only where the KB could have said
# TRUE, and the comment says what would flip it.

:expect-pins 20

# ── "Keeping one is permitted for a declared purpose and for nothing else." ─
? complete(RecordSequenceAuthorization, ReviewedRecordHolding, RecordSequenceSubject).
# => TRUE

? authority(RecordSequenceHolder, DeliverTheServiceRecorded, RecordSequenceAuthorization).
# => TRUE

# "accurate and correctable on request ... and open to the person it is about"
? obliged(RecordSequenceHolder, KeepRecordAccurateCorrectableAndSecure, RecordSequenceAuthorization).
# => TRUE

# "retaliation for objecting" is one of the defects the family names, and the
# duty not to retaliate is a conclusion rather than an aspiration.
? obliged(RecordSequenceHolder, ProtectRecordSubjectsAndObjectorsFromRetaliation, RecordSequenceAuthorization).
# => TRUE

# ── "Watching is a separate permission, and it points at the exact holding
# that was reviewed." ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
? permits(RecordSequenceHolder, OrdinaryRecordKeeping, RecordSequenceRecord).
# => TRUE

? complete(RecordSequenceRecord, ReviewedRecordProcessing, RecordSequenceSubject).
# => TRUE

# "and notice to the person afterwards"
? obliged(RecordSequenceHolder, NotifyTheSubjectAfterCovertProcessing, RecordSequenceRecord).
# => TRUE

# ── "Letting a machine help is a permission of its own, and the design keeps
# it as help." ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# "There has to be an explanation of what went in and how it was reasoned"
? obliged(RecordSequenceHolder, ExplainAutomatedSupportAndAllowContest, RecordSupportRecord).
# => TRUE

# "a real chance to contest before anything takes effect"
? permits(RecordSequenceSubject, ContestAutomatedSupport, RecordSupportRecord).
# => TRUE

# "a human and independent review that actually decides"
? obliged(RecordSupportReviewer, DecideWithHumanAndIndependentReview, RecordSupportRecord).
# => TRUE

? complete(RecordSupportRecord, ReviewedAutomatedDecisionSupport, RecordSequenceSubject).
# => TRUE

# ── "None of it says anything about the person." ───────────────────────────
# The subject of a holding is an opaque handle, not a roster entry: keeping a
# record about somebody does not put them on the roster, and this is the
# direction that matters — it cannot take them off it either. Writing a birth
# or an encounter for this handle is what would make personhood derive, and
# the record family supplies neither.
? person(RecordSequenceSubject).
# => FALSE

? false(RecordSequenceSubject).
# => FALSE

? prisoner(RecordSequenceSubject).
# => FALSE

? lose(Points, RecordSequenceSubject).
# => FALSE

# And nobody else in the record moves either.
? owe(State, Eats, Hano).
# => TRUE

? decide(Hano, Ballot).
# => TRUE

# ── The child with nobody ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# "The floor does not run through a file" — no record about this child exists
# and every debt holds anyway.
? person(Nell).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Healthy, Nell).
# => TRUE

# "an entry naming this child would be an entry, not a finding" — and no such
# entry has been written, so nothing about the child is held at all.
? false(Nell).
# => FALSE
